    story: `One of the fascinating parts of Jaipur beyond its majestic forts & palaces is the secret corners where some of the world's finest artisans practise the exquisite arts & crafts of Jaipur. Known as a "shoppers' paradise", Jaipur is home to world-class artisans famed for their traditional & unique way of crafting timeless treasures. Whether you are looking for fine jewellery, textiles, blue pottery, marble statues, rugs, lac bangles, or brass & copper artefacts, everything is crafted here with distinctive quality & innovation.\n\nBut "Artisan's Jaipur" is definitely not a shopping tour; rather, it is a curated walking experience around Jaipur's art & craft. The walk encompasses several old city lanes & streets that were dedicated exclusively to particular professions and were named after them. The walk starts around one of the "Chaupars" of Jaipur, the grand commercial hubs of their time, around which everything was built—be it temples, havelis, schools, shops, community squares or markets. Badi Chaupar is the point of intersection of major streets that have been, and continue to be, the shopping hub for weddings, festivals & every celebration, as it is the perfect place to get traditional Indian women's attire like sarees, suits, lehengas and the finest jewellery.\n\nDuring the walk, you will come across local jewellers who have been trusted by families for many generations. You may be fascinated by their shop arrangements, their way of doing business and the safe places where they keep valuable metals & stones. Continuing ahead, we take a look at the narrow lanes of the local textile markets, which meet the needs of almost every citizen of Jaipur. Further on, we might also explore the bustling lane for precious & semi-precious stones, where we can marvel at the local trading by dealers & jewellers. By entering these lanes, you will realise how massive the market is and why the Pink City is called the "Gem Capital of India". Almost every resident of the old walled city is connected with Jaipur's jewellery, directly or indirectly.\n\nFrom there, we move ahead to explore the "brass & copper merchants' lanes", followed by the "printers of Jaipur", in a small cluster of bustling streets. These artisans are known for making artefacts that are mostly used in temples as adornments and as basic utensils for worship. Besides, the "printing press" is another dominant profession in the city, where family-run businesses make almost everything out of paper, and wedding invitations are the best amongst them. Taking our walk ahead, you will also admire the fascinating art of crafting fine bangles in surprising colours & designs with lac resin. Once you enter here, it feels like entering a different world, where the jewels of these bangles, their vibrant colours, unique designs and the generous families making them capture your attention. While exploring these lanes, do not forget to take a look at another narrow lane where we can see trending wedding lehengas, suits & sarees that form the attire of an Indian woman.\n\nAs part of the experience, the journey takes you through aromatic streets filled with the scent of freshly ground spices, traded quietly yet extensively throughout the market. Before concluding the walk, do not forget to marvel at the exquisite craftsmanship, patience and precision of the artisans putting life into marble, chiselling raw rock into timeless sculptures through centuries-old techniques.`,
